## Role
Cyprus employment layer for individuals, relocated remote workers and small employers, often with a permit or tax regime in play. Common law plus EU statute; the **Industrial Disputes Court** (Δικαστήριο Εργατικών Διαφορών, IDC) and the **Redundancy Fund** decide outcomes.

## Primary Sources
- **TEL** = Termination of Employment Law, N. 24/1967 — dismissal, notice, probation, redundancy, Fund.
- Annual Holidays with Pay Law, N. 8/1967 — leave; **s.12 establishes the IDC** and its deadlines.
- Working Time N. 63(I)/2002; Wages N. 35(I)/2007; Minimum Wages Cap. 183 + Orders 2022/2025.
- Transparent and Predictable Working Conditions N. 25(I)/2023 (Dir. (EU) 2019/1152); Social Insurance N. 59(I)/2010; GESY N. 89(I)/2001; Maternity N. 100(I)/1997; Equal Treatment N. 58(I)/2004; Trade Unions N. 71/1965.
- Contract Law Cap. 149 s.27; Limitation N. 66(I)/2012; Cap. 105 (permits); Brussels I bis (EU) 1215/2012 arts 20–23; Rome I art.8.

## Contract Types & Mandatory Written Terms
- Indefinite by default; fixed-term N. 98(I)/2003 (a chain may be recharacterised, TEL s.5(d)); part-time N. 76(I)/2002.
- **The contract need not be written**; N. 25(I)/2023 s.11(1) requires written essential terms, s.13(1) a document on change, s.11(3)(ζ) probation.
- **There is also a live filing duty, routinely missed**: under s.11(6), Κ.Δ.Π. 455/2024 as amended by Κ.Δ.Π. 58/2025 requires every employer to register all employees' essential terms in the **ERGANI** system, the window having run to 31 May 2025.

## Probation & Early Termination
- TEL s.3(1): compensation needs **≥26 weeks' service** — no TEL claim below it, which dominates every argument; first proviso, a **written contract made at recruitment** may extend it **to 104 weeks**.
- TEL s.9(1) provisos: on probation **no notice is due** if either side ends it; **no probation exceeds 104 weeks**; beyond **26 weeks** it needs **written agreement at the start of employment**. s.9(2): employment is presumed **not** probationary until the employer proves otherwise.
- **Conflict, and the file had it backwards:** N. 25(I)/2023 s.15(1) caps probation at **6 months**, and the words „ανεξαρτήτως οποιωνδήποτε ρυθμίσεων ίσχυαν πριν από την έναρξη της ισχύος του παρόντος Νόμου“ **extend** that cap to arrangements already in force — they do not exempt later ones, and the prohibition is unqualified as to time. It transposes Dir. (EU) 2019/1152 art.8(1); s.28(1) preserves only terms **more favourable** to the employee, so TEL s.9(1)'s 104 weeks is not saved by it, and s.31 repeals only the information-on-terms Law, which is why the consolidated TEL still prints 104 weeks. **Treat the 6-month cap as governing**, subject to s.15(3) (managing executives), s.15(4) (extension by the period of absence) and the fixed-term proportionality proviso. TEL s.3(1)'s 104-week extension is a *qualifying-service* threshold, not probation, and is not obviously caught **(unconfirmed: no ruling opened)**.

## Working Time, Rest & Leave
- N. 63(I)/2002: **48 h/week average incl. overtime** (s.7(1)), reference period **4 months** (s.7(3)); rest **11 h** daily (s.4(1)), **24 h** weekly (s.6(1)); night work **8 h/24 h** (s.9(1)).
- Leave **4 weeks** minimum (s.8(1)), cash in lieu only on termination (s.8(2)); N. 8/1967 s.5(1): **20 working days** (five-day week) or **24** (six-day) after **≥48 weeks** worked that leave year.
- **Central Holiday Fund** — the statutory name in N. 8/1967 s.8(1) is **Κεντρικόν Ταμείον Αδειών**; *Ταμείο Ετησίων Αδειών μετ' Απολαβών* is the Social Insurance Services' administrative name. Employer-only contributions set by Regulations under s.9(2)(β), and the rate is **proportional to the leave entitlement: 8% at 4 weeks, rising to 16% at 8 weeks**. 8% is the floor, not the rate. Exempt employers pay none; the 2026 ceiling applies.
- **No general statutory overtime premium, statutory public holidays or employer sick pay.** N. 59(I)/2010 s.31(1) first proviso pays no sickness benefit for the **first 3 days** — for everyone. The **9-day** bar applies only to a person insured through article 11 periods, that is the **self-employed**, and is lifted by an accident or at least one overnight hospital stay. Maximum **156 days** per interruption (s.31(3)). Quoting 9 days to an employee overstates the gap by six days.

## Pay, Minimum Wage & Mandatory Bonuses
- Cap. 183 s.3(1): minimum wages fixed **by Order in the Gazette**; s.5(1) duty to pay no less; **s.11 burden on the employer**.
- Minimum Wage Orders 2022/2025, **from 1 January 2026**: **€979 gross/month on recruitment**, **€1,088 after six months' service with the same employer**; pro rata part-time.
- Introduced **from 1 January 2023** by the 2022 Order; initial amounts recalled as €885 rising to €940 **(unconfirmed** — Order not opened**)**. Excluded: **domestic, agricultural, livestock, seafaring**; hotels have a separate 2025 Order.
- **No general statutory overtime multiplier**, and the Department of Labour Relations requires only that overtime be paid at **at least 1:1** and the rate be stated in the terms of employment — but the sector statutes do impose real multipliers, and they differ. **Shops:** N. 155(I)/2006 **s.18(1)** sets **1:2** for overtime on a Sunday and on the s.15 free mornings or afternoons and **1:1½** on other days, and **s.18(2)** sets **1:2** for a shop employee who works, with consent, one of the **fifteen paid holidays in s.16** or the Sunday forming part of his weekly rest; s.18 sits in Part III, which **s.3(1)** applies to **all shops and all shop employees**, only the opening-hours Part IV being territorially limited. **Recreation centres:** **Reg. 3(3)(β)** of the Schedule to N. 80/1968 pays each overtime hour **as one and a half**, Reg. 3(3)(α) capping overtime at **8 hours a week** (10 exceptionally), and the Ministry's own page gives **1:1½** for κέντρα αναψυχής and for hotels — so **do not carry the shop 1:2 across to a hotel or a recreation centre**. Whether any Κ.Δ.Π. under s.6 of N. 80/1968 adds a holiday list and a 1:2 for recreation centres is *(unconfirmed — the Department's consolidated PDF could not be read)*.
- **No statutory 13th salary or COLA (ΑΤΑ)** — contractual or collective only; N. 8/1967 s.12(1)(e) lets the IDC hear "δέκατο τρίτο μισθό" as such a right.

## Exclusivity, Moonlighting & Non-Compete
- **In service:** N. 25(I)/2023 s.16 (Dir. (EU) 2019/1152 art.9) — no **prohibition or penalty** for work with other employers **outside contracted hours**; restriction only **at hiring, in writing, with concrete objective reasons**. The **duty of fidelity** remains.
- **Post-termination:** Cap. 149 s.27(1) — an agreement restraining a lawful profession, trade or business is **void**; exceptions (s.27(2)): **goodwill sale**, **partnership**, **not employment**. Statutory voidness, not the English reasonableness test; any judicial gloss **(unconfirmed)**. Treat non-competes as presumptively void.

## Termination: Grounds, Procedure, Severance
- **Unlawful dismissal**, TEL s.3: a reason outside s.5, employee **≥26 weeks**. First Schedule — at least the redundancy equivalent (para. 2), **never above two years' wages** (para. 3). **s.3(2): employer pays up to one year's wages, the Fund the excess.**
- **Redundancy**, TEL s.16(1): **≥104 weeks** with the same employer, termination by redundancy as defined in **s.18** → payment **from the Fund, not the employer**, on the Fourth Schedule.
- Employer notice, s.9(1), by weeks of service: 26–51 **1 week**; 52–103 **2**; 104–155 **4**; 156–207 **5**; **208 to fewer than 259** → **6**; **260 to fewer than 311** → **7**; **312 or more** → **8**; **in writing** (s.9(5)). Employee notice, s.10: **1 / 2 / 3 weeks**.
- Read paragraphs (ε) and (στ) literally: they run *to fewer than* 259 and 311, so weeks 259 and 311 themselves fall in an unlegislated gap. Do not paper over it with a tidy "208–259" band.
- Fourth Schedule, weeks' wages per year: years 1–4 **2**; 5–10 **2.5**; 11–15 **3**; 16–20 **3.5**; 21–25 **4** — ceiling **25 years ≈ 75.5 weeks**; **lapses 52 weeks after termination** (s.16A(4)).
- The cap is **4× weekly basic insurable earnings**, and Κ.Δ.Π. 378/2025 fixes that weekly amount at **€220.76**, so pay above **€883.04 a week** is disregarded. No 2026 Order was found, so re-check the figure for 2026.
- **The base is otherwise missing from that scale.** "Weeks' wages" needs fixing before a number comes out of it: which pay elements count, the reference period for variable pay and commissions, and how a part year is treated. This file does not settle it **(unconfirmed)**.
- **Automatically unfair grounds**, TEL s.6(2) ("never valid reasons"): union activity, representative role, bona fide complaint, **pregnancy or maternity**, family leave, and the s.6(2)(δ) list — a **closed list of eight**: race, colour, sex, family status, religion, political opinions, national origin, social descent.
- **Age, disability and sexual orientation are not in that list.** They run through N. 58(I)/2004, so do not reach them via TEL s.6(2).
- Pregnancy ban: N. 100(I)/1997 s.4(1)(α) protects an employee who notified in writing **from the start of pregnancy until five months after maternity leave**; s.4(3) **revokes** a dismissal made in ignorance of it, on a certificate within **five working days**.
- That protection opens "Με την επιφύλαξη των διατάξεων του άρθρου 4Β", and **s.4Β disapplies it** for serious misconduct, cessation of the business, and expiry of a fixed term — the last unless non-renewal is linked to the pregnancy or the maternity leave. State the ban with its carve-outs or it reads as absolute.

## Collective Bargaining & Employee Representation
- **No statute makes collective agreements directly enforceable.** Bargaining runs on the voluntary tripartite **Industrial Relations Code of 1977**; agreements are traditionally treated as **"gentlemen's agreements"** **(unconfirmed: secondary commentary only)**.
- Practical route: **incorporation** into the individual contract, expressly or by custom — N. 8/1967 s.12(1)(e) then lets the IDC enforce it as a right from "law, regulation, custom, individual or collective agreement"; s.12(1)(c) allows ministerial referral **to the Industrial Disputes Court**, not to arbitration; decrees can bind sectors, as in hotels.
- Whether any statutory works council or board-level representation exists is **(unconfirmed)** here: no instrument was opened either way, so do not assert the negative.
- **Collective redundancies are a separate regime** the rest of this section does not reach: N. 28(I)/2001 sets the headcount and period thresholds in s.2 and the information, consultation and notification duties in ss.4(1) and 6(1). Check it before advising on any multi-employee termination.
- **Finding the instrument**: the Department of Labour Relations publishes an **Ηλεκτρονικό Αρχείο Συλλογικών Συμβάσεων**, an electronic archive of collective agreements, so query it first — it is not a register of coverage, so still ask for the contract, the staff handbook and any union recognition document, and establish whether a sectoral decree binds the employer — as one does in hotels. Absent incorporation, a collective agreement gives the worker nothing enforceable.

## Social Security & Payroll
| Contribution, as published by the Social Insurance Services (its page states no year) | Employee | Employer | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| Social Insurance Fund (22.8%) | 8.8% | 8.8% | 5.2% |
| Redundancy Fund | — | 1.2% | — |
| Human Resource Development Fund | — | 0.5% | — |
| Social Cohesion Fund | — | 2% | — |
| GESY, N. 89(I)/2001 s.19(1) | 2.65% | 2.90% | 4.70% |
- Insurable-earnings ceiling **2026: €1,325/week from 5 Jan 2026, €5,742/month from 1 Jan 2026** (four employment funds). GESY stops above **€180,000** aggregate income (s.19(4)(a)).
- **Discrepancy:** N. 59(I)/2010 s.5(1)(στ) schedules **8.9%/8.9%/5.3%** from the 2024 contribution year, but its provisos allow actuarially lower rates; the applied 2026 rate is **8.8%/8.8%/5.2%**. The employer owes **both** contributions (s.5(5)).
- **Side activity:** the self-employed rate is fixed by **s.12 of N. 59(I)/2010**, not by s.5, which governs employed persons only; the Law schedules **16.8% plus 5.3% State** from the 2024 contribution year against the **16.6% plus 5.2%** applied. GESY **4.00%** (s.19(1)(γ)), on notional income bands **(figures unconfirmed)**; GESY is charged **again** on that income, the record supporting s.19(4)(a) for the charge; **"liability runs from registration" has no support in s.19** and is not asserted here *(unconfirmed)*; rent, dividends, interest **2.65%** (s.19(1)(ζ)). For a permit holder, registration is also an immigration event.

## Foreign Workers & Permit Coupling
- **Constitutional personal scope is not settled in this file (unconfirmed)**: no constitutional article was opened, so anchor a foreign worker's position in the TEL and the other employment statutes, which turn on weeks of service rather than nationality, and in Cap. 105 — not in a constitutional labour right.
- Work is immigration permission, not a labour right: Cap. 105 s.14Β(1) makes it an offence to employ an alien **without the required permit or in breach of its terms**. The permit names **employer and post**, so changing either needs fresh approval.
- On dismissal the permit's basis falls away, **but not by operation of law**: Cap. 105 s.18ΦΓ(9) preserves the Director's **power to revoke or refuse renewal**, and s.18Φ(1) makes that decision one that must be reasoned and that is subject to recourse. So there is something to challenge, and a pending IDC claim is not it. **Family permits are derivative.** Grace periods **(unconfirmed: Migration Department pages returned HTTP 403)**.
- **Companies of foreign interests / Business Facilitation Unit**: **€2,500/month** gross for highly skilled staff, third-country staff capped at **70%** over five years, confirmed from the official strategy and its Q&A. The reduced **€2,000** grace figure those parameters rest on **expires 31 December 2026**, which this file outlives within weeks — re-verify before quoting it for 2027.
- **EU Blue Card**, Cap. 105: filed **through the employer** (s.18Τ(1)); salary threshold set by ministerial Order at **≥1× the average gross annual salary** (s.18ΣΕ(2)) — amount **(unconfirmed)**.
- Blue Card mobility: in the **first 12 months** an employer change needs an application and is **suspended up to 30 days** for checks (s.18ΤΓ(2)–(3)); later, notification alone (s.18ΤΓ(4)). Revoked once unemployment exceeds **3 months** (card held under two years) or **6 months** (s.18ΤΔ(1)). No IDC claim suspends these clocks.

## Dispute Procedure: Forum, Deadlines, Burden of Proof
- **Forum:** the IDC, established by **N. 8/1967 s.12(1)**, exclusive over disputes under that Law, disputes referred by any other law, **autonomous contract claims (leave, accrued wages, gratuity, 13th salary)**. TEL s.30(1) confirms exclusivity; **s.30(2)** allows the District Court for larger claims.
- **Composition, s.12(2):** a President or Judge **plus two lay Members** for the employer and labour sides; **the President's view on law binds them** (s.12(2)(c)); procedure is summary (s.12(11)).
- **Deadlines, s.12(10A): 12 months from the date the right to apply arose**, or **9 months from the Redundancy Fund's reply** for a redundancy payment. Appeal **on law only, within 42 days** (s.12(11A)); the text names the Supreme Court; the post-2023 forum **(unconfirmed)**.
- **Other claims:** N. 66(I)/2012 — contract **6 years** (s.7(1)) from completion of the cause of action (s.3), extension up to **2 years** (s.22). Under the TEL the 12-month deadline bars instead.
- **Nothing here says what suspends the 12 months**, and the Ministry of Labour mediation offered below is practical rather than statutory. Whether mediation interrupts or suspends that deadline is **(unconfirmed)**: assume it keeps running and file in time, because the mediation is recoverable and the lapsed deadline is not.
- **Relocation tax relief is not covered here**, though this file is written for relocated workers: the non-domicile and expatriate-income reliefs sit in `skill-jurisdiction-cy`. Establish which applies, and what paid side activity does to it, before advising — taking side work can cost a regime as easily as it earns the fee.
- **Burden of proof: TEL s.6(1)** presumes an admitted dismissal was **not** for an s.5 reason, so the **employer** proves the ground and any probation (s.9(2)); the employee bears constructive dismissal (s.7(2)); discrimination reverses onto the respondent (N. 58(I)/2004 s.11); and the minimum-wage burden of **Cap. 183 s.11 falls only in a prosecution** (δίωξη) — do not carry it into a civil claim.
- **Remedies:** compensation **up to two years' wages** (employer the first year, Fund the excess, s.3(2)); redundancy payment from the Fund; **reinstatement** only under TEL s.3(1)'s second proviso — **more than 19 employees**, dismissal **manifestly unlawful, or unlawful and in bad faith** (έκδηλα παράνομος ή παράνομος και κακόπιστος) — bad faith alone does not qualify, employee requested it. Ministry of Labour mediation is practical, not statutory **(unconfirmed)**.
- **A foreign forum clause fails for a worker habitually working in Cyprus; a choice of law does not:** Brussels I bis arts 21–22 and **art.23** (a departing agreement is valid only post-dispute or if it widens the employee's choice). **Rome I art.8(1) upholds the chosen law** and then caps it: the choice may not have the result of depriving the employee of the protection of provisions that cannot be derogated from under the law that would otherwise apply, **art.8(2)–(4)** — for habitual work in Cyprus, Cypriot law. So plead the clause as valid and compare it provision by provision against the Cypriot non-derogable floor; do not plead that it fails. Art.9 is separate — **9(1) defines an overriding mandatory provision; 9(2) is the operative rule** that lets the forum apply its own.

## Non-Waivable Rights & Void Clauses
- No single TEL anti-waiver clause — voidness is provision-specific. **s.9(4):** a provision for **shorter notice** is **void**; **s.14(2):** a term restricting that section's effect is **void**; **s.20A(1):** any **assignment or charge of a redundancy payment** is **void**.
- Redundancy payment comes **from the Fund** (s.16(1)), so a contractual waiver of it has nothing to operate on; payment in lieu only under s.11(1).
- N. 25(I)/2023 **s.28**: more favourable statutory or collective provisions are unaffected — a floor, not a ceiling. N. 8/1967 s.5(1) proviso: longer leave from law, custom or agreement **replaces** the statutory days; N. 35(I)/2007 s.11: no assignment of wages.

## Common Pitfalls
1. Treating the first 26 weeks as protected, or missing a written probation extension toward 104.
2. Confusing unlawful dismissal with redundancy: 26 vs 104 weeks, different payers and deadlines.
3. Pleading a wage claim as a contract claim and missing the 12-month IDC deadline.
4. Importing English restraint-of-trade reasoning instead of Cap. 149 s.27(1).
5. Relying on a collective agreement without incorporation.
6. Forgetting the permit: employer- and post-tied, derivative family permits, Blue Card clocks.

## Citation Format
- `s.3(1) of the Termination of Employment Law, N. 24/1967`, Greek title on first use; `s.27(1) of the Contract Law, Cap. 149`; `para. 3 of the First Schedule`.
- Decree: `the Minimum Wage Order of 2025 (Κ.Δ.Π. …)`, the number only if verified. EU: `art. 23 of Regulation (EU) No 1215/2012`. Case: `Name v Name (year) 1 A.A.Δ. page`.
- **Never cite an unopened judgment**; mark anything unverified `(unconfirmed)`.

